{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs realized prices, especially beyond ~1 week; apply a downward level shift (≈2–4 €/t early horizon, widening to ≈6–10 €/t by weeks 3–4).",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: in sustained sell-offs the model declines too slowly and misses acceleration; increase negative slope when recent 5–10d momentum is strongly negative (i.e., extrapolate downside more aggressively).",
    "Over-optimistic mean reversion: model repeatedly expects rebounds back toward prior range that do not materialize; dampen upward corrections after drawdowns (reduce mean-reversion strength) unless confirmed by positive momentum/volatility contraction.",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: mid-to-long horizon forecasts drift upward relative to reality even when short-horizon is close; enforce horizon-based bias correction that grows with lead time.",
    "Downside tail underestimation: realized paths show deeper troughs than forecast; widen downside adjustments when recent volatility is elevated and prices are making lower lows (add extra negative convexity in weeks 2–4)."
  ]
}
